what is the difference between postgreSQL and mysql ?
answer :- both are relational databases
          postgresql -> compliant , stable and most mature database
                        very good with complex queires
                        advanced object-relational database , but mysql has traditional relational database , columns can be stored in more complex structures.

           MVCC -> multi version concurrency control
                     means the database keeps multiple versions of rows so readers can access old committed data
                     while writers create new versions , reducing locks and improving concurrency
                     it is cleaned by using vacuum in postresql.
                     done by using both postgresql and mysql.
                     in mysql -> old versions are reconstructed using undo logs.
                     but , in postgresql , the different versions are stored directly.

